WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.780
- When you think of editing in music,

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:05.640
you might imagine auto-tuning
the vocals of a pop singer

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.050
or modifying the rhythm of the drums.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:09.450
Many people don't realize

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:12.340
that editing exists in
classical recordings as well.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.230
- For example, watch these two clips

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:16.070
of concert pianist Krystian Zimerman

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:17.894
playing Chopin's "Ballade No. 1."

00:00:17.894 --> 00:00:19.921
Can you find evidence of an edit?

00:00:19.921 --> 00:00:22.588
(piano playing)

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.210
It's not in what you
heard, but what you saw.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.440
Notice the change in the
bench he's sitting on,

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.572
implying that there were multiple takes.

00:00:37.572 --> 00:00:40.000
(upbeat music)

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Many listeners have been
surprised to discover

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.990
that this performance wasn't
recorded in a single take.

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:46.517
And one commenter wrote,

00:00:46.517 --> 00:00:48.700
"Since the chair was a dead giveaway,

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.630
I wonder how many clips were actually used

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:52.070
to make this recording."

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:54.430
- Now listen to this track
and see if you can find

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:56.444
any evidence of edits.

00:00:56.444 --> 00:00:58.501
♪ Boy yeah right, yeah right, yeah right ♪

00:00:58.501 --> 00:01:00.611
♪ Boy yeah right, yeah right, yeah right ♪

00:01:00.611 --> 00:01:04.480
♪ Free something that's erased ♪

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:05.530
- [Narrator] In that clip,

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:08.200
everything from the auto-tune vocals

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.270
to the electronic drum
kit is heavily processed.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.740
But people are less
critical of the editing

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.160
in electronic music.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:16.720
- So why is there a double standard

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.700
and how much editing is
acceptable in classical music?

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.367
(piano playing)

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.890
First of all, why do musicians
edit their recordings?

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.570
There are two distinct
reasons why musicians edit.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:31.830
Number one, to erase mistakes
and strive for perfection,

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:34.440
and number two, for stylistic choices.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.900
For example, auto-tune,
which adjusts the pitch

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.690
of a recording to correct
out of tune singing

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.740
is an example of editing
to erase mistakes.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:44.860
But sometimes, auto-tune is used

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:46.320
for the other type of editing

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.902
and is entirely a stylistic choice.

00:01:48.902 --> 00:01:50.659
♪ I got money in the bank ♪

00:01:50.659 --> 00:01:52.296
♪ Shawty, what you think 'bout that? ♪

00:01:52.296 --> 00:01:54.453
♪ I'll be in the gray Cadillac ♪

00:01:54.453 --> 00:01:55.286
♪ We in the bed ♪

00:01:55.286 --> 00:01:57.310
Because this is somewhat
of a subject of topic,

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:59.522
we invited a group of
respected classical musicians

00:01:59.522 --> 00:02:02.310
to discuss their opinions on editing.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:05.163
So the basis of what we
want to talk about today is

00:02:05.163 --> 00:02:08.427
everything in relation to editing

00:02:08.427 --> 00:02:10.810
in the context of classical music.

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:14.420
- I love being on panels
because I feel like I learn

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:16.150
from everybody so much

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:18.670
and probably won't learn anything from me

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:21.630
but I will have a wonderful
time hearing what all

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:22.613
of you have to say.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.210
- Before we dive into the debate

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.070
on when editing goes too far,

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:28.000
let's cover the history

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.060
on how we got here in the first place.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:33.480
In 1877, Thomas Edison created the first

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.720
reliable recording device, the phonograph.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.310
Over the next 60 years,

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:41.450
music was stored on
discs and wax cylinders

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.300
and couldn't be edited.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:44.360
Errors were prevalent

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.930
because musicians had to
record each piece in one take.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:51.340
For example, listen for the
mistake in Alfred Cortot's

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:54.479
1933 recording of a Chopin etude.

00:02:54.479 --> 00:02:57.146
(piano playing)

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:02.820
- Even without the ability to edit,

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.920
some classical musicians
were critical of recording.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.960
After one 1932 studio session,

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:10.250
pianist Artur Schnabel said

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:13.050
that recording music
was a surrender to evil,

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:15.371
a betrayal of life in
which the human being,

00:03:15.371 --> 00:03:18.080
the original, is forgotten.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.120
In the 1940s,

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.760
the development of magnetic
tape allowed musicians

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.940
to splice together different
takes of the same piece.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:25.870
This is around the time

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:28.610
that pianist Glenn Gould
enters the picture.

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:31.470
Unlike some, Gould fully
embraced recording.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.337
He even retired from public performances

00:03:33.337 --> 00:03:38.030
at the young age of 32 to
focus on recording full time.

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:40.810
He thought recordings were
the future of classical music

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:43.480
and claimed that the concert is dead.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:45.780
But not everyone was as
excited about the ability

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:47.940
to splice together different takes.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.270
With each new innovation,

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:52.740
there is a period of resistance
followed by acceptance.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.203
Just like Schnabel
criticized recording before,

00:03:55.203 --> 00:03:58.430
other musicians shared
their criticisms of editing.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:03:59.687
Here's a book I discovered called

00:03:59.687 --> 00:04:01.870
"Recording the Classical Guitar"

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:04.970
and it says Andres Segovia
didn't like performances

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:06.176
to be spliced.

00:04:06.176 --> 00:04:08.800
He always felt guilty when
there was a certain amount

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:10.670
of splicing because he felt
he should have been able

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:14.840
to play the pieces note
perfect the first time.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:17.890
Then it says here by the mid 1960s,

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.000
this attitude towards splicing began

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.412
to change as artists
and producers recognized

00:04:22.412 --> 00:04:25.200
that recorded performances
were not obliged

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.930
to serve as reflections
of live performance,

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.880
a development which coincided
with a dramatic increase

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.440
in the facilities of studio
technology for editing sound.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.013
Just an open question to start off with.

00:04:37.013 --> 00:04:41.310
How much editing do you
think is an okay amount?

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:44.200
Is there such a thing in
your personal practice

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:48.910
and your experience as a musician?

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:51.630
- My opinion on this
topic is that, you know,

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:55.220
minor adjustments to just
erase that little pimple

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:58.380
without changing the shape of, you know

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.600
all the other features on your face

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.780
- I mean, if I had my way
there would be zero editing.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:06.370
I mean, I have edited my
stuff for sure, anyway,

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:09.880
but it's sort of like
the effect of, sort of,

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:11.765
Instagram on photos.

00:05:11.765 --> 00:05:14.810
That kind of playing
can't be replicated live.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:16.840
And that's also what we're expecting

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.140
because that's what we hear.

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:19.416
- It's a slippery slope when you try

00:05:19.416 --> 00:05:21.030
to make something so perfect,

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:22.560
and then when somebody
comes to your concert,

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:23.920
they're like, "Ooh," you know.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:26.460
It didn't sound like
that on the recording.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.810
I think at some point you
will have to figure out

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:30.700
what the line is for you personally.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.700
I don't think you should
be playing all 88 keys

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:35.500
and then just putting
together a Beethoven sonata

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:36.420
from those tones.

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:39.540
I mean I think that's ridiculous
but that's an extreme.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:42.114
- Since the 1980s,
digital recording methods

00:05:42.114 --> 00:05:44.208
have taken over the music industry

00:05:44.208 --> 00:05:45.570
- What is the process

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:49.260
of editing such a thing

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:50.780
for string musicians?

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:51.780
- In terms of what can be edited,

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:53.560
it could be timing, it
could be intonation,

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:54.530
It can be sound quality.

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:56.349
It can be even placing in different notes,

00:05:56.349 --> 00:05:58.020
bar by bar, note by note.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:05:59.630
I mean, if you master

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:02.190
and then you put it to this
program called Melodyne

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:05.209
and then it's polyphonic
intonation algorithm.

00:06:05.209 --> 00:06:06.511
Then you can adjust each note.

00:06:06.511 --> 00:06:09.730
- The thing you have to make sure of,

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:11.301
the producer that you work with

00:06:11.301 --> 00:06:15.010
knows what kind of performer you are

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:20.060
on stage and what kind of
rendition you're trying

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:21.070
to get on the disc

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:23.990
so that that trust between,
I think, the artist

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:27.270
and the producer is very important.

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:28.900
- Some advancements have been made,

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:30.500
but editing remains minimal

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:33.170
and splicing is still the primary tool.

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:34.003
Why is that?

00:06:34.003 --> 00:06:37.270
Well, classical music usually
isn't tied to a rhythmic grid

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:39.540
and the tempo is often bent.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:42.240
For example, listen to
the slight tempo changes

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:45.278
in Johannes Brahms's Hungarian
Rhapsody Number Five.

00:06:45.278 --> 00:06:48.611
(classical music plays)

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:00.860
Subtle shifts in tempo and
phrasing make it impractical

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:03.159
to record and edit parts separately.

00:07:03.159 --> 00:07:06.210
As a result, it's easier
to run larger chunks

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:08.990
as a full ensemble and then use splicing

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:10.630
to get rid of mistakes.

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:13.040
So if editing happens
less in classical music

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:16.220
than in pop music, why
is it controversial?

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:19.600
- One reason is that pop
music is often recorded first

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:21.350
and then performed later.

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:23.002
Classical music is flipped.

00:07:23.002 --> 00:07:25.460
It was initially written to be performed

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:28.230
but is now also recorded.

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:31.470
For the audience, the classical
recording is secondary

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:33.710
and it's just a representation
of what they would hear

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:35.650
if they saw the performance live.

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:37.510
That means editing out mistakes made

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:41.220
inaccurately enhance the
performance actual ability.

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:42.920
For example, one editor

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.820
on the Audio Asylum music
board claimed he knew

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:49.158
of a classical album that
had as many as 900 edits

00:07:49.158 --> 00:07:52.160
or once every four to five seconds.

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:56.030
- I've helped produce albums which have

00:07:56.030 --> 00:07:57.683
over a thousand of edits,

00:07:58.757 --> 00:07:59.590
classical ones.

00:07:59.590 --> 00:08:01.669
So there is this wide range, you know

00:08:01.669 --> 00:08:06.669
five edits for one person and
thousands for another, so.

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:08.600
- Even more dishonest is the practice

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:12.470
of editing quote, unquote, live concerts.

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:15.728
Often they are multiple live
performances stitched together,

00:08:15.728 --> 00:08:17.180
dress rehearsals

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:19.820
and additional audience
free recording sessions.

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:22.970
Sometimes whole movements
from a classical music CD

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:24.931
were never even performed live.

00:08:24.931 --> 00:08:28.250
So how does editing
affect music listeners?

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:31.160
Around 50% of people who
stream classical music

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:33.150
listen to it in the background.

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:36.060
However, serious listeners might dislike

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:37.520
that current recording methods

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:39.960
capture a limited volume range.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:41.549
This is fine for pop music

00:08:41.549 --> 00:08:46.210
but classical music usually
has wider dynamic swings.

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:49.360
Listen for how the quiet
French horn transforms

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:52.750
into the massive coral theme
in Beethoven's Ninth Symphony

00:08:52.750 --> 00:08:54.252
in a matter of seconds.

00:08:54.252 --> 00:08:57.585
(classical music plays)

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:02.210
Naxos, the largest classical music label

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:05.220
has considered using different
forms of audio compression

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:08.636
to better capture the dynamic
range of classical music.

00:09:08.636 --> 00:09:11.200
- What about aspiring classical musicians?

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:12.200
In classical music,

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:15.770
there is a culture around
reaching unattainable perfection.

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:18.200
With the advancement
in editing technology,

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:21.130
that standard has been raised even higher.

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:24.387
Mental health is a common
issue among young musicians.

00:09:24.387 --> 00:09:26.970
One recent study found that one-third

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:29.970
of Spanish music students
have used substances

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:32.390
to cope with performance anxiety.

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:35.060
An increased standard
of perfection puts more

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:38.830
and more pressure on an already
competitive field of study.

00:09:38.830 --> 00:09:42.410
- And I don't think it's
affecting our industry positively

00:09:42.410 --> 00:09:46.000
because it's changing what is normal,

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:47.430
'cause again the most normal thing

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:49.710
is to put on a recording,
not go to a concert.

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:51.353
That's an event for everybody.

00:09:51.353 --> 00:09:53.220
- On the other hand, the prevalence

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:56.420
of high quality recordings
allows music students to learn

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:57.813
from the greatest performers

00:09:57.813 --> 00:10:00.140
with the click of a button.

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:02.430
Compare this to the 20 year old JS Bach,

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:04.884
who traveled 250 miles on foot,

00:10:04.884 --> 00:10:08.860
to listen to the brilliant
organist Dieterich Buxtehude.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:11.490
The expectation for
mistake free recordings

00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:14.410
also puts a lot of pressure
on recording musicians.

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:17.569
It's rumored that pianist
Krystian Zimerman took 76 takes

00:10:17.569 --> 00:10:21.410
of Liszt's B minor Sonata
before he was satisfied.

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:23.390
However, editing allows artists

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:25.980
to better capture their
vision for a composition.

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:28.800
Glenn Gould likened
himself to a film producer,

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:32.879
meticulously combining his takes
into unique interpretation.

00:10:32.879 --> 00:10:34.900
Maybe transparency is key.

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:37.230
By openly sharing the
extent of their edits,

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:39.965
classical musicians can
embrace the new opportunities

00:10:39.965 --> 00:10:41.900
of modern editing.

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:43.600
At the same time, they acknowledge

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.356
that the recording has less mistakes

00:10:45.356 --> 00:10:47.540
than what they would realistically create

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:49.020
in a public performance.

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:52.991
- I think it's also good to
remind, at least myself, right?

00:10:52.991 --> 00:10:56.149
Our main audience is not some
studio class at Julliard.

00:10:56.149 --> 00:10:57.938
It's the general public.

00:10:57.938 --> 00:11:00.397
When we put out things and
we have really tiny mistakes,

00:11:00.397 --> 00:11:03.700
it's like sometimes I'm like,
"Oh, we made a tiny mistake.

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:05.630
Everybody will know about it."

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:06.680
That's just not true.

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:10.430
Our main audience is the general public.

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:13.620
- As technology improves,
editing in classical music

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:15.400
may become more sophisticated.

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:17.620
One composer wrote a piece that was faster

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:20.550
than one Boston ensemble
could humanly perform.

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:24.020
So he had the orchestra
record the piece at half tempo

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:27.420
and later doubled the speed
with editing software.

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:29.850
The final recording was convincing enough

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:31.640
that a New York ensemble couldn't tell

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:33.030
that edits had been made.

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:35.250
- Some may argue that
with editing like this

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:38.910
we cut out the imperfections
that make art human.

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:40.460
With AI generated art,

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:42.932
the human element is removed completely.

00:11:42.932 --> 00:11:46.110
Some AI art pieces are very convincing.

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:48.090
Recently editors at The Archive

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:50.430
at Duke University Literary Journal

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:53.075
accepted an AI generated poem.

00:11:53.075 --> 00:11:57.210
Or consider this AI generated
piece that mimics Bach.

00:11:57.210 --> 00:11:59.493
In one survey, 50% of people,

00:11:59.493 --> 00:12:02.700
most of whom were classical
music enthusiasts,

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:06.126
couldn't tell the difference
between the Bach and the AI.

00:12:06.126 --> 00:12:09.626
(classical music playing)

00:12:13.460 --> 00:12:16.470
Another advancement in
editing technology are VSTs

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:18.010
or virtual instruments.

00:12:18.010 --> 00:12:20.280
These virtual instruments
aren't commonly used

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:21.870
in classical music today

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:24.180
but maybe there'll be more
accepted in the future.

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:26.950
Some VSTs sounds so
realistic that it's hard

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:29.270
to tell them apart from real instruments.

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:31.240
And to put this to the test,

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:33.170
Arthur and I faced off in a quiz

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:35.660
to see who could guess
which instruments were real

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:37.596
and which were virtual.

00:12:37.596 --> 00:12:39.573
(applause)

00:12:39.573 --> 00:12:42.442
- Let's, let's, let's
start with Piano G chord.

00:12:42.442 --> 00:12:45.849
- I'm playing mine.
(piano chord plays)

00:12:45.849 --> 00:12:47.766
- They both sound fake.

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:50.787
Okay, I'm ready, I'm ready.

00:12:50.787 --> 00:12:51.620
Are you ready?
- Here we go.

00:12:51.620 --> 00:12:52.453
- [Both] One, two, three.

00:12:52.453 --> 00:12:55.050
- Okay. I think we on the same page.

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:59.356
- Oh we got it right.
- Good, good, good, good.

00:12:59.356 --> 00:13:00.189
- Drums one and two.

00:13:00.189 --> 00:13:02.856
(drums playing)

00:13:11.733 --> 00:13:14.203
- When I get this wrong, Imma be ashamed

00:13:14.203 --> 00:13:16.188
that you can't tell
what's a real drum like.

00:13:16.188 --> 00:13:20.500
- I hear this shh sound
that is just way to perfect

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:22.250
- The China cymbal, yeah the China.

00:13:23.150 --> 00:13:24.490
- Okay, lets do this.

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:28.980
One, two, three.
- Two, three.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:30.007
Oh.

00:13:30.007 --> 00:13:34.507
(cheering)
(laughing)

00:13:35.824 --> 00:13:38.540
I knew I was gonna get
that wrong, that's crazy.

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:39.749
- Let's see orchestra.

00:13:39.749 --> 00:13:42.666
(orchestral music)

00:13:48.018 --> 00:13:49.601
- Naw, it's like...

00:13:51.090 --> 00:13:52.510
- This might be a trap.

00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:53.890
- I'm analyzing, hold on a second.

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:55.663
- Two, three.

00:13:57.130 --> 00:13:59.920
What? Wait, no way.
(laughing)

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:02.070
This is the absolute nonsense.

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:03.020
I quit.

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:04.113
This is so, what?

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:06.910
- Should we do cymbal one, cymbal two?

00:14:07.924 --> 00:14:08.757
- Yes.
- I think this one

00:14:08.757 --> 00:14:09.731
might be low key easy.

00:14:09.731 --> 00:14:14.731
(cymbals clanging)
(chuckling)

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:28.640
At this point I'm literally
like, swinging in the dark.

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:31.200
I do not know.

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:32.033
- I want to know.

00:14:32.033 --> 00:14:33.760
Okay, two, three.

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:34.593
- Okay, what we on?

00:14:34.593 --> 00:14:36.713
This is the first time we agree.

00:14:36.713 --> 00:14:37.739
- We got it wrong.

00:14:37.739 --> 00:14:39.638
No way, why?

00:14:39.638 --> 00:14:41.042
Why?

00:14:41.042 --> 00:14:42.497
How?

00:14:42.497 --> 00:14:44.207
- This game is cheating.

00:14:44.207 --> 00:14:46.312
- No, no, let's do piano, piano.

00:14:46.312 --> 00:14:49.701
- All right Nahre, hey, no pressure.

00:14:49.701 --> 00:14:52.999
No pressure, but you're
making history here.

00:14:52.999 --> 00:14:54.460
(piano playing)

00:14:54.460 --> 00:14:55.423
Aw, come on, come on.

00:14:56.306 --> 00:14:57.552
- Why is it "Fur Elise?"

00:14:57.552 --> 00:14:59.272
- Right? Right?

00:14:59.272 --> 00:15:01.522
(laughing)

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:03.540
This one was easy.

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:05.253
- Two, Three.

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:08.513
One real, no.

00:15:09.781 --> 00:15:12.031
(laughing)

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:14.880
- What?

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:17.628
- Nahre, I got drums wrong.

00:15:17.628 --> 00:15:20.523
You got orchestra, classical music wrong.

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:24.002
What are we doing?

00:15:24.002 --> 00:15:24.850
(sad music)

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:28.220
So, Nahre, what made you
want to do an episode

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:29.420
on editing in classical music?

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:30.760
What really pushed your button

00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:33.320
to make this whole episode go?

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:36.790
- What's interesting to me
is that if you're recording,

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:38.766
you're in a completely
different environment

00:15:38.766 --> 00:15:41.620
and you're performing in a different way.

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:44.460
First of all, you're
entitled to do many takes.

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:46.260
You're not just gonna go in there,

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:48.192
play one take and then leave.

00:15:48.192 --> 00:15:49.025
- And keep it.

00:15:49.025 --> 00:15:50.700
- That in and of itself is editing

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:52.234
because you're doing, I dunno,

00:15:52.234 --> 00:15:55.010
10, 20, 30, 40 takes, whatever it is,

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:57.540
then that's a form of editing already.

00:15:57.540 --> 00:15:59.160
So if we're doing that,

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:02.323
and it's a completely
different form of art,

00:16:03.710 --> 00:16:06.510
why be so shy about other edits?

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:09.690
But at the same time, I
recognize that this process

00:16:09.690 --> 00:16:11.290
has some consequences as well

00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:15.810
because now we're pushing
what perfection is.

00:16:15.810 --> 00:16:20.170
So later on, when you're on the stage,

00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:23.080
you have two standards
that you're up against,

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:25.640
the recorded standard
and the stage standard.

00:16:25.640 --> 00:16:27.410
And that kind of plays with your head

00:16:27.410 --> 00:16:28.972
and I feel like that's an interesting,

00:16:28.972 --> 00:16:33.972
yet at times, disturbing
part of being a musician

00:16:34.884 --> 00:16:36.390
in the studio or on stage.

00:16:36.390 --> 00:16:38.733
So that's why I was drawn to this episode.

00:16:38.733 --> 00:16:41.260
(applause)

00:16:41.260 --> 00:16:43.260
A huge shout out to The Cliburn,

00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:45.730
whose mission is to advance
classical piano music

00:16:45.730 --> 00:16:46.840
throughout the world

00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:49.260
for partnering with us on this video.

